1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:03,374
Sampling, measurement and ring-width series.

2
00:00:03,829 --> 00:00:08,447
This explanation follows the information from collection to chronology.

3
00:00:08,908 --> 00:00:14,208
Pause for the exercises corresponding to the ninety-minute classroom lesson.

4
00:00:15,011 --> 00:00:17,222
Plan sampling and records.

5
00:00:17,684 --> 00:00:21,217
Explain a sampling plan and the metadata it needs.

6
00:00:21,671 --> 00:00:23,974
Measure widths from boundaries.

7
00:00:24,434 --> 00:00:29,434
Check the path, spatial calibration, boundary positions and units.

8
00:00:29,895 --> 00:00:32,007
Combine ring-width series.

9
00:00:32,461 --> 00:00:36,178
Distinguish detrending, tree weighting and sample depth.

10
00:00:36,632 --> 00:00:42,124
A central aim is to keep every value traceable to a specimen and measurement path.

11
00:00:42,927 --> 00:00:45,099
Question and sampling plan.

12
00:00:45,561 --> 00:00:48,488
Specimens, images and records.

13
00:00:48,942 --> 00:00:51,186
Observe and measure boundaries.

14
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:54,100
Crossdating and quality control.

15
00:00:54,561 --> 00:00:56,758
Detrending and chronology.

16
00:00:57,219 --> 00:01:00,646
Return to the wood and records when a mismatch appears.

17
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:03,752
Preserve information at each stage.

18
00:01:04,206 --> 00:01:09,176
A final graph alone does not allow its measurement decisions to be checked.

19
00:01:09,979 --> 00:01:11,164
Climate.

20
00:01:11,613 --> 00:01:16,896
Choose species, sites and comparison areas relevant to the climate variable.

21
00:01:17,350 --> 00:01:18,948
Forest history.

22
00:01:19,402 --> 00:01:23,981
Plan how spatial sampling will capture disturbance and regeneration.

23
00:01:24,442 --> 00:01:26,396
Buildings and objects.

24
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:32,422
Record member location, working or reuse, and surviving inner and outer wood.

25
00:01:32,876 --> 00:01:38,521
The event of interest determines which positions, preserved edges and comparison material

26
00:01:38,534 --> 00:01:40,068
are needed.

27
00:01:40,871 --> 00:01:42,392
Increment core.

28
00:01:42,847 --> 00:01:48,169
One radial record; document sampling position, losses and injuries.

29
00:01:48,623 --> 00:01:50,235
Disc or block.

30
00:01:50,689 --> 00:01:55,775
Allows comparison across radii and scars; define the sampled extent.

31
00:01:56,229 --> 00:01:57,788
Surface image.

32
00:01:58,242 --> 00:02:03,255
Records the readable surface; requires a scale and imaging conditions.

33
00:02:03,715 --> 00:02:07,051
Cross-sections and cores preserve different views.

34
00:02:07,505 --> 00:02:12,904
Plan whether the available material can answer the question before collecting it.

35
00:02:13,707 --> 00:02:15,879
Did the core reach the pith?.

36
00:02:16,183 --> 00:02:18,683
Is the outermost ring preserved?.

37
00:02:18,986 --> 00:02:22,657
Could bending, breakage or compression affect the record?.

38
00:02:22,959 --> 00:02:27,367
A feature absent on one core may survive elsewhere around the tree.

39
00:02:27,828 --> 00:02:30,908
Record position and orientation.

40
00:02:31,711 --> 00:02:33,363
Identification.

41
00:02:33,819 --> 00:02:38,990
Link site, tree, specimen and path IDs to images and containers.

42
00:02:39,450 --> 00:02:41,384
Collection and condition.

43
00:02:41,845 --> 00:02:48,036
Date, species, member position, height, direction, pith, bark and losses.

44
00:02:48,490 --> 00:02:50,522
Processing and measurement.

45
00:02:50,976 --> 00:02:57,055
Preparation, image calibration, operator, boundaries, revisions and reasons.

46
00:02:57,516 --> 00:03:02,963
Use a consistent identifier to connect photographs, collection records and measurements,

47
00:03:03,134 --> 00:03:07,296
while retaining the contextual information behind the number.

48
00:03:08,099 --> 00:03:12,949
You want to study moisture variability in a forest with ridges and valleys.

49
00:03:13,404 --> 00:03:17,503
Choose sites, trees, radii and the records to keep.

50
00:03:17,957 --> 00:03:22,226
State one limitation arising from the places you did not sample.

51
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:28,891
Pause and specify sample numbers, positions and comparisons, including an alternative if the

52
00:03:28,904 --> 00:03:31,852
preferred material is unavailable.

53
00:03:32,655 --> 00:03:34,695
Identify immediately.

54
00:03:35,157 --> 00:03:40,565
Link specimen and container IDs; retain orientation and fragment order.

55
00:03:41,026 --> 00:03:42,822
Handle for the condition.

56
00:03:43,276 --> 00:03:48,105
Record cracks, decay and moisture; minimise distortion and mixing.

57
00:03:48,565 --> 00:03:50,605
Record the initial state.

58
00:03:51,065 --> 00:03:55,677
Save photographs and collection records, including missing material.

59
00:03:56,131 --> 00:04:01,815
Losing order or bark-side orientation can make later interpretation much harder, even when

60
00:04:01,828 --> 00:04:04,208
the wood itself survives.

61
00:04:05,011 --> 00:04:06,597
Mount and orient.

62
00:04:07,053 --> 00:04:11,651
Support the core after checking its orientation and cellular structure.

63
00:04:12,105 --> 00:04:13,836
Prepare the surface.

64
00:04:14,290 --> 00:04:18,980
Use preparation suited to the material to reveal boundaries and cells.

65
00:04:19,434 --> 00:04:21,165
Inspect the result.

66
00:04:21,619 --> 00:04:26,013
Distinguish scratches and preparation marks from narrow increments.

67
00:04:26,474 --> 00:04:31,395
Prepare a readable surface without losing the features needed for interpretation, and

68
00:04:31,408 --> 00:04:35,028
document how preparation altered the specimen.
